{
    "cells": [
     {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
       "### 1. Data Preparation\n",
       "In this first part, we will explore our dataset and clean it. The dataset we will use contains excerpts from Donald Trump's speeches during his 2016 campaign."
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
       "**(a)** Using the `pandas` package, import the data contained in the file `\"trump.csv\"` into a `DataFrame` named `df` using the following separator : `'|'`. \n",
       "\n",
       "**(b)** Display the first 10 rows of the dataset."
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
       "import pandas as pd\n",
       "\n",
       "# (a) Import the data\n",
       "df = pd.read_csv(\"trump.csv\", sep=\"|\")\n",
       "\n",
       "# (b) Display the first 10 rows\n",
       "df.head(10)"
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
       "**Output Description:** The code displays the first 10 rows of the DataFrame, showing the 'Speech' and 'Sentiment' columns."
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
       "**(c)** Create a string named `speeches` containing the concatenation of all entries in the `\"Speech\"` column of `df`. Be sure to insert a space between each line."
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
       "# (c) Concatenate all speeches into a single string\n",
       "speeches = \" \".join(df[\"Speech\"].astype(str))"
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
       "**Output Description:** This cell creates a single string variable named `speeches` containing all the text from the 'Speech' column. There is no direct output displayed."
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
       "In the text, the audience reactions are indicated by square brackets, `[Applause]` for example.\n",
       "\n",
       "**(d)** Create a `test` variable containing the string: `Hello World! [Applause] How are you ?`.\n",
       "\n",
       "**(e)** Using the `re` library, create a function named `remove_brackets` which takes as input a string and replaces the words in square brackets with spaces.\n",
       "\n",
       "**(f)** Apply this function to `test` to check its effectiveness."
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
       "import re\n",
       "\n",
       "# (d) Create a test string\n",
       "test = \"Hello World! [Applause] How are you ?\"\n",
       "\n",
       "# (e) Define a function to remove text in brackets\n",
       "def remove_brackets(text):\n",
       "    return re.sub(r'\\[.*?\\]', '', text)\n",
       "\n",
       "# (f) Apply the function to the test string\n",
       "print(remove_brackets(test))"
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
       "**Output Description:** The output shows the test string with the `[Applause]` part removed, verifying the function works correctly."
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
       "**(g)** Apply the `remove_brackets` function to the `speeches` string."
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
       "# (g) Apply the function to the main speeches string\n",
       "speeches = remove_brackets(speeches)"
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
       "**Output Description:** This cell cleans the main `speeches` string by removing all bracketed text. No output is displayed."
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
       "**(h)** Using the `nltk` library, create a variable named `stop_words` containing the common stop words of the English language and some additional custom words and characters."
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
       "import nltk\n",
       "from nltk.corpus import stopwords\n",
       "\n",
       "# (h) Create a set of stopwords\n",
       "# nltk.download('stopwords') # Uncomment and run this line if you haven't downloaded stopwords yet\n",
       "stop_words = set(stopwords.words('english')).union({\n",
       "    \"?\", \"!\", \".\", \",\", \":\", \";\", \"-\", \"--\", \"...\", \"\\\"\", \"'\", \n",
       "    \"they've\", \"they're\", \"they'll\", \"i've\", \"i'm\", \"i'll\", \"could\"\n",
       "})"
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
       "**Output Description:** This cell defines a set of words that will be filtered out from the text. No output is displayed."
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
       "**(i)** Import the `TweetTokenizer` class from the `nltk.tokenize` package.\n",
       "\n",
       "**(j)** Instantiate the `TweetTokenizer` class in a `tokenizer` object.\n",
       "\n",
       "**(k)** Apply the `tokenize` method of `tokenizer` to `speeches` and store the result in a variable named `tokens`."
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
       "from nltk.tokenize import TweetTokenizer\n",
       "\n",
       "# (i, j) Instantiate the tokenizer\n",
       "tokenizer = TweetTokenizer()\n",
       "\n",
       "# (k) Tokenize the speeches string\n",
       "# nltk.download('punkt') # Uncomment and run this line if you haven't downloaded the tokenizer models yet\n",
       "tokens = tokenizer.tokenize(speeches)"
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
       "**Output Description:** The `speeches` string is broken down into a list of individual words (tokens). No output is displayed."
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
       "**(l)** Display the total number of words as well as the number of different words found in these speeches."
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
       "# (l) Print total and unique word counts before filtering\n",
       "print(f\"Total words: {len(tokens)}\")\n",
       "print(f\"Unique words: {len(set(tokens))}\")"
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
       "**Output Description:** The output shows the total count of all words (70180) and the count of unique words (4938) in the speeches before filtering."
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
       "**(m)** Remove all `stop_words` from the `tokens` list and display the new total word count."
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
       "# (m) Filter out stopwords from the tokens list\n",
       "tokens = [word for word in tokens if word.lower() not in stop_words]\n",
       "\n",
       "# Display the new counts\n",
       "print(f\"Total words after filtering: {len(tokens)}\")\n",
       "print(f\"Unique words after filtering: {len(set(tokens))}\")"
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
       "**Output Description:** The output shows the new total word count (28383) and unique word count (4679) after removing the common stop words."
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
       "### 2. Wordcloud Construction\n",
       "In this section we will build a wordcloud from the `tokens` variable."
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
       "**(a)** Import the `WordCloud` class.\n",
       "\n",
       "**(b)** Instantiate the `WordCloud` class with specific parameters.\n",
       "\n",
       "**(c)** Display the wordcloud using `matplotlib.pyplot`."
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
       "import numpy as np\n",
       "from PIL import Image\n",
       "from wordcloud import WordCloud\n",
       "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
       "\n",
       "# Load the mask image\n",
       "mask = np.array(Image.open(\"trump.jpg\"))\n",
       "\n",
       "# (a, b) Instantiate the WordCloud object\n",
       "wc = WordCloud(\n",
       "    background_color=\"white\",\n",
       "    max_words=1000,\n",
       "    max_font_size=90,\n",
       "    collocations=False,\n",
       "    random_state=42,\n",
       "    mask=mask\n",
       ")\n",
       "\n",
       "# Generate the wordcloud from tokens\n",
       "wc.generate(\" \".join(tokens))\n",
       "\n",
       "# (c) Display the wordcloud\n",
       "plt.figure(figsize=(10, 10))\n",
       "plt.imshow(wc, interpolation=\"bilinear\")\n",
       "plt.axis(\"off\")\n",
       "plt.show()"
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
       "**Output Description:** An image of a word cloud is displayed, shaped like the provided `trump.jpg` mask. The most frequent words in the speeches appear largest. The words are in default colors on a white background."
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
       "**(d)** Import the `ImageColorGenerator` class.\n",
       "\n",
       "**(e)** Instantiate an `ImageColorGenerator` object.\n",
       "\n",
       "**(f)** Use the `recolor` method to apply the image's colors to the wordcloud.\n",
       "\n",
       "**(g)** Remove the axes from the graph.\n",
       "\n",
       "**(h)** Display the wordcloud again."
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
       "from wordcloud import ImageColorGenerator\n",
       "\n",
       "# (d, e) Create a color generator from the mask image\n",
       "img_color = ImageColorGenerator(mask)\n",
       "\n",
       "# (f) Recolor the wordcloud using the image colors\n",
       "wc.recolor(color_func=img_color)\n",
       "\n",
       "# (g, h) Display the final, recolored wordcloud\n",
       "plt.figure(figsize=(10, 10))\n",
       "plt.imshow(wc, interpolation=\"bilinear\")\n",
       "plt.axis(\"off\")\n",
       "plt.show()"
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
       "**Output Description:** A new word cloud image is displayed. It has the same shape and words as before, but now the colors of the words are taken from the original `trump.jpg` image."
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
       "### 3. Sentiment Analysis\n",
       "In this part, we will try to create a sentiment analysis model on this dataset."
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
       "**(a)** Import the `train_test_split` function.\n",
       "\n",
       "**(b)** Create a `Series` `X` and a `Series` `y`.\n",
       "\n",
       "**(c)** Split the data into training and testing sets."
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
       "from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n",
       "\n",
       "# (b) Define features (X) and target (y)\n",
       "X = df[\"Speech\"]\n",
       "y = df[\"Sentiment\"]\n",
       "\n",
       "# (c) Split the data\n",
       "X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(X, y, test_size=0.25, random_state=20)"
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
       "**Output Description:** The data is split into training and testing sets for both features (`X`) and the target variable (`y`). No output is displayed."
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
       "**(d)** Import the `CountVectorizer` class.\n",
       "\n",
       "**(e)** Instantiate the `CountVectorizer` class.\n",
       "\n",
       "**(f)** Apply the `fit_transform` and `transform` methods on the data."
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
       "from sklearn.feature_extraction.text import CountVectorizer\n",
       "\n",
       "# (d, e) Instantiate the vectorizer\n",
       "vectorizer = CountVectorizer()\n",
       "\n",
       "# (f) Vectorize the text data\n",
       "X_train_vec = vectorizer.fit_transform(X_train)\n",
       "X_test_vec = vectorizer.transform(X_test)"
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
       "**Output Description:** This cell converts the text data into a numerical format (a matrix of token counts) that machine learning models can understand. No output is displayed."
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
       "**(g)** Train a `DecisionTreeClassifier`.\n",
       "\n",
       "**(h)** Train a `GradientBoostingClassifier`."
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
       "from sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeClassifier\n",
       "from sklearn.ensemble import GradientBoostingClassifier\n",
       "\n",
   t    "# (g) Train a Decision Tree model\n",
       "decision_tree = DecisionTreeClassifier()\n",
       "decision_tree.fit(X_train_vec, y_train)\n",
       "\n",
       "# (h) Train a Gradient Boosting model\n",
       "gradient_boosting = GradientBoostingClassifier()\n",
       "gradient_boosting.fit(X_train_vec, y_train)"
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
       "**Output Description:** The two classification models are trained on the vectorized training data. The output shows the default parameters of the `GradientBoostingClassifier`."
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
       "**(i)** Using the accuracy on the test set, compare the performance of the two algorithms."
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
       "from sklearn.metrics import accuracy_score\n",
       "\n",
       "# (i) Predict and calculate accuracy for both models\n",
       "dt_pred = decision_tree.predict(X_test_vec)\n",
       "gb_pred = gradient_boosting.predict(X_test_vec)\n",
       "\n",
       "dt_acc = accuracy_score(y_test, dt_pred)\n",
       "gb_acc = accuracy_score(y_test, gb_pred)\n",
       "\n",
       "print(f\"Decision Tree Accuracy: {dt_acc:.4f}\")\n",
       "print(f\"Gradient Boosting Accuracy: {gb_acc:.4f}\")"
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
       "**Output Description:** The accuracy scores for both models are printed. The `GradientBoostingClassifier` performs better than the `DecisionTreeClassifier`."
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
       "**(j)** Display the confusion matrices of these two algorithms on the test set. Which sentiment is difficult to detect?"
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
       "from sklearn.metrics import confusion_matrix\n",
       "import seaborn as sns\n",
       "\n",
       "# Calculate confusion matrices\n",
       "dt_cm = confusion_matrix(y_test, dt_pred)\n",
       "gb_cm = confusion_matrix(y_test, gb_pred)\n",
       "\n",
       "# Get class labels\n",
       "labels = sorted(y_test.unique())\n",
       "\n",
       "# Plot Decision Tree Confusion Matrix\n",
       "plt.figure(figsize=(6, 5))\n",
       "sns.heatmap(dt_cm, annot=True, fmt=\"d\", cmap=\"Blues\", xticklabels=labels, yticklabels=labels)\n",
       "plt.title(\"Decision Tree Confusion Matrix\")\n",
       "plt.xlabel(\"Predicted\")\n",
       "plt.ylabel(\"Actual\")\n",
       "plt.show()\n",
       "\n",
       "# Plot Gradient Boosting Confusion Matrix\n",
       "plt.figure(figsize=(6, 5))\n",
       "sns.heatmap(gb_cm, annot=True, fmt=\"d\", cmap=\"Greens\", xticklabels=labels, yticklabels=labels)\n",
       "plt.title(\"Gradient Boosting Confusion Matrix\")\n",
       "plt.xlabel(\"Predicted\")\n",
       "plt.ylabel(\"Actual\")\n",
       "plt.show()"
      ]
     },
     {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
       "**Output Description:** Two heatmaps are displayed, representing the confusion matrices for each model.\n",
       "* **Top-left to bottom-right diagonal:** Correct predictions.\n",
       "* **Off-diagonal:** Incorrect predictions.\n",
       "\n",
       "By comparing the off-diagonal values, we can see how each sentiment is being misclassified. Both models have more trouble correctly identifying the **Negative** sentiment compared to the Positive one. Therefore, **'Negative' is the more difficult sentiment to detect**."
      ]
     }
